# Design note
A single tapered oval load path grows from the elbow flange into a fork, with a straight internal cable passage.
I tried a split-rail arm; it saved mass but looked like two exposed struts rather than a continuous form.
The first thin root deflected too much, and a growth-from-inside flange still concentrated stress at the held joint.
A wider shoulder improved the exploratory response but made an obvious ledge beneath the disc.
A 1 mm radius softens its edge; strength and pin-axis thrust are not established.
With another hour I would rebuild the elbow junction as a truly tangent transition and model the real bolted and thrust-bearing hardware, then converge the local stresses.
The least satisfying part is the elbow shoulder: it still looks like a separate collar, and its held boundary dominates the study.
